Recurrent Balanoposthitis (Secondary to Undiagnosed/Unmanaged Diabetes)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Recurrent glans/foreskin erythema, Thick white curdy exudate, Acquired phimosis, Elevated HbA1c

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-037
Reason for Visit: Exquisite penile pain, massive swelling, and an inability to retract the foreskin, combined with profound systemic thirst and fatigue, requiring immediate intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient in extreme localized distress with severe genital erythema, acquired phimosis, and active fissuring, alongside classic systemic symptoms of uncontrolled diabetes. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically required to relieve the severe localized pain, exclude ischemic paraphimosis or necrotizing fasciitis, and screen for severe hyperglycaemia precluding delay to regular in-hours care.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin on my penis is completely swollen shut, it burns like fire, and there is a horrible thick white discharge coming from underneath.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports recurrent episodes of severe erythema and swelling of the glans penis and foreskin over the past six months, which have acutely escalated to an intolerable level tonight. They describe intense localized pruritus and a severe burning sensation. A thick, white, curdy exudate has accumulated beneath the prepuce. There is a progressive inability to retract the foreskin, leading to acquired phimosis. They note a sweet, characteristic foul odour from the accumulated smegma and report severe dyspareunia prior to the complete swelling. Systemically, they report severe polyuria, intractable polydipsia, and profound lethargy over the same six-month period.
Functional Status: Independent, but walking with a wide stance to avoid friction against the severely inflamed genitalia.
Social/Safety Concerns: High distress due to the sensitive nature of the symptoms and severe systemic exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.1C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 105 Kg
General: Obese, visibly uncomfortable, frequently requesting water due to profound systemic thirst.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: Examination reveals severe erythema and oedema of the entire prepuce and glans. The foreskin cannot be retracted due to acquired fibrotic phimosis. Excoriations and small superficial fissures are visible on the distal preputial ring. A thick, white, curdy exudate is visible at the meatus. Cardiovascular: Capillary refill is normal; no signs of spreading systemic cellulitis or necrosis. Point-of-care capillary blood glucose is markedly elevated at 22.4 mmol/L. Expected investigation results include visualization of pseudohyphae on a potassium hydroxide preparation of the discharge and a significantly elevated HbA1c.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty large bottles of sugary soft drinks visible in the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications; patient has not seen a doctor in several years.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Recurrent Balanoposthitis (Secondary to Undiagnosed/Unmanaged Diabetes) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Paraphimosis - Ruled out/unlikely because the foreskin is stuck in the forward, unretracted position, not trapped behind the coronal sulcus causing acute ischemia.

Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of the classic thick, curdy, white exudate typical of candidal infection, rather than spreading hot, red, purely bacterial inflammation.

Sexually Transmitted Infection (e.g., Gonorrhoea) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the specific fungal characteristics of the discharge, the acquired phimosis, and the overwhelming concurrent systemic signs of severe hyperglycaemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from a severe, acute fungal infection of the genitalia directly driven by severe, newly discovered, uncontrolled diabetes mellitus. The profound hyperglycaemia has immunosuppressed the patient and filled the urine with glucose, creating a perfect environment for massive candidal overgrowth leading to acquired phimosis. An urgent after-hours physical examination was absolutely mandatory to perform a point-of-care blood glucose test, definitively exclude a surgical emergency like paraphimosis or Fournier's gangrene, and initiate immediate aggressive antifungal and anti-inflammatory therapy. Deferring care would have resulted in unmanageable pain, worsening phimotic scarring, and potential diabetic ketoacidosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a combination topical cream containing clotrimazole 1 percent and hydrocortisone 1 percent to aggressively treat the fungal overgrowth and reduce the severe acute inflammation causing the phimosis. Administered a stat dose of oral Fluconazole 150mg. Ordered urgent fasting morning bloods including HbA1c, comprehensive metabolic panel, and formal urine culture.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate healing and manage the severe localized inflammation, the patient is instructed to wash the area daily using only lukewarm water, explicitly avoiding all soaps, shower gels, or scrubbing. The rapid reduction in burning and pain achieved by eliminating chemical irritants will positively reinforce this gentle hygiene routine. Furthermore, to address the profound hyperglycaemia, they are instructed to immediately replace all sugary beverages with plain water.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review tomorrow afternoon to review the urgent fasting glucose and HbA1c, and formally commence diabetic management.

